{"table":"nodes","ref":"sha256:c941bb8e2efcb2c545fdebbfec6f904193275455192bec48bcb452c5b8ab7f97/nodes/scalar.source_predictable_lower","record":{"id":"scalar.source_predictable_lower","kind":"claim","label":"Predictable orthonormal correlations are bounded by the ground-state value","status":"unverified","source_id":"scalar-r6-source-predictable-lower","locator":"ConstantFatou.lean:48–134; OAI.SKValueG.predictable_coefficients_le_groundState","statement":"For fixed K, measurable predictable f_j with finite second moments, unit L2 norms and pairwise orthogonality for j<K, and any measurable sign S, the sum over j<K of E[f_j S] E[S z_(j+1)] is at most groundStateValue. No centring or minimizing coefficient is required.","assumption_ids":[],"evidence_ids":[],"citation_ids":["scalar.source_predictable_lower_lean_source"],"boundary":"Source-inspected conditional statement with quantified guards retained in the statement. The source proof is present; no fresh elaboration, dependency extraction, Comparator result or independent semantic acceptance is asserted. The expectation-limit argument adds a nonnegative correction before passing to the limit; signed convergence alone would not suffice."},"usage":{"record_ref":"sha256:c941bb8e2efcb2c545fdebbfec6f904193275455192bec48bcb452c5b8ab7f97/nodes/scalar.source_predictable_lower","paper_focus":[],"paper_memberships":[{"stage":"long","subgraph_ref":"sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json/support/0.3.1/69473417dc487cafa5e2e39b3168d2c4a8bffdad2e91b6e486de48f122dda071","roles":["mapped_claim"]},{"stage":"short","subgraph_ref":"sha256:b3fb22a55f09aba18f53cd9bbac859142355ee389bba5aa7bf98d016dc611a43/papers/short.json/support/0.3.1/77f0cf29ab577b42d8caa5b2831ebfb1ca170d4b13c1ef6897cadc8e93c2a834","roles":["recorded_prerequisite"]},{"stage":"expository","subgraph_ref":"sha256:082c1187f897999ee934fa70dbb519221ed1e0aebc169fac91b7211a4a599dc5/papers/expository.json/support/0.3.1/9dd5536bc567fc55efaa864733bf89747cea0268a8a3865dec51ddd8e2a235dd","roles":["recorded_prerequisite"]}],"direct_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:9e0f6d255eb0a116c513a0c11e94d28559f0a429ef365c3137bb4ff3b6a061d6/papers/long.md/sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json/passages/formal-lower-overview","anchor":"formal-lower-overview","mapping_ref":"sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids"},{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:9e0f6d255eb0a116c513a0c11e94d28559f0a429ef365c3137bb4ff3b6a061d6/papers/long.md/sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json/passages/gaussian-expectation-limit","anchor":"gaussian-expectation-limit","mapping_ref":"sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids"},{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:9e0f6d255eb0a116c513a0c11e94d28559f0a429ef365c3137bb4ff3b6a061d6/papers/long.md/sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json/passages/heat-lower-bound","anchor":"heat-lower-bound","mapping_ref":"sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids"}]},"aligned_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:9e0f6d255eb0a116c513a0c11e94d28559f0a429ef365c3137bb4ff3b6a061d6/papers/long.md/sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json/passages/formal-lower-overview","anchor":"formal-lower-overview","mapping_ref":"sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids","text":"\n<a id=\"independent-scalar-lower-bound\"></a>\n\n### 10.2 A universal bound for predictable correlations\n\nThe scalar implementation obtains the value equality by two inequalities.\nIts finite-spin limit follows from superadditivity and a uniform upper\nbound. A finite-profile Gaussian comparison supplies\n$P_*\\le\\mathcal P(\\gamma)$ for every admissible coefficient. The reverse\ninequality at a minimizer comes from a more general finite-dimensional\nfact: predictable orthonormal Gaussian functions paired with any spin\nhave a shifted correlation sum at most $P_*$. We reconstruct that fact\nbelow. It supplies the inequality that §7's mesh limit consumes, without\ninvoking the literature's Parisi formula used in §6.\n\nWrite $e_n=n^{-1}\\mathbb E\\max_\\sigma H_n(\\sigma)$, with the Hamiltonian\nof §1. The finite-spin convergence and upper comparison are separate\ninputs to this subsection; their precise suppliers are\n[the superadditive limit][scalar-spin-limit] and\n[the upper comparison][scalar-upper]. The proof below concerns the\nlower comparison, not a finite-disorder algorithm or a circuit.\n\n**Proposition 10.1 (predictable Gaussian comparison).** Fix $K<\\infty$, independent standard normals $z_0,\\ldots,z_K$,\nand real functions $f_j=f_j(z_0,\\ldots,z_j)$ for $0\\le j<K$.\nAssume measurability, square integrability and\n$\\mathbb E[f_if_j]=\\delta_{ij}$. For any measurable\n$S=S(z_0,\\ldots,z_K)\\in\\{-1,1\\}$, the conclusion is\n$$\n\\sum_{j=0}^{K-1}\\mathbb E[f_jS]\\,\\mathbb E[S z_{j+1}]\\le P_*.\n$$\nThese assumptions allow nonconstant, dependent $f_j$; even\n$\\mathbb E f_j=0$ is unnecessary. Their order of dependence ensures\n$\\mathbb E[f_i z_{j+1}]=0$ whenever $i\\le j$. The next fresh coordinate,\nrather than centring of $f_i$, is what will remove the projection terms.\n[Scalar implementation, predictable-coefficient theorem][scalar-predictable].\n","span":{"start_byte":170048,"end_byte":171892,"start_line":3623,"end_line":3657},"paper_href":"/api/v1/papers/long?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb","reading_href":"/api/v1/papers/reading?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&paper=long&passage=formal-lower-overview","query_href":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&paper=long&passage=formal-lower-overview"},{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:9e0f6d255eb0a116c513a0c11e94d28559f0a429ef365c3137bb4ff3b6a061d6/papers/long.md/sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json/passages/gaussian-expectation-limit","anchor":"gaussian-expectation-limit","mapping_ref":"sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids","text":"\n<a id=\"gaussian-expectation-limit\"></a>\n\n#### 10.2.4 Passing the expectation bound and returning to the value\n\nSince $S^2=1$, $\\|\\widehat S_n\\|=1$ for $n>0$, and every residual\nprojection contracts. The column formula gives\n$|E_{n,j}|\\le1+\\|\\widehat z_{j+1,n}\\|^2$.\nDefine\n$$\nc_n=\\sum_{j<K}(1+\\|\\widehat z_{j+1,n}\\|^2).\n$$\nThen $\\sum_jE_{n,j}+c_n\\ge0$, $\\mathbb E c_n=2K$ exactly, and\n$c_n\\to2K$ almost surely. Fatou's inequality, the finite bound and\n$e_n\\to P_*$ imply\n$$\n\\sum_{j<K}a_jb_{j+1}+2K\n\\le\\liminf_n\\mathbb E\\bigl[\\sum_{j<K}E_{n,j}+c_n\\bigr]\n\\le P_*+2K.\n$$\nCancelling $2K$ proves the claimed predictable-coefficient inequality.\nThe source's constant-limit helper establishes the same nonnegative\ncomparison by clipping at the limiting constant and applying dominated\nconvergence. It shifts to dimensions $n+1$ to avoid dimension zero.\nIt does not apply Fatou directly to the signed energies or presume\nuniform integrability. [Column bound and correction][scalar-empirical-bound];\n[expectation limit and final theorem][scalar-predictable].\n\nFor the Euler construction in §7, take $K=N$, relabel its $Z_{j+1}$ as\n$z_j$, and set $f_j=g_{j+1}$. Each normalized increment depends only\non $z_0,\\ldots,z_j$ and the increments are orthonormal when their raw\nsecond moments are positive. The rounded sign is spin-valued almost\nsurely: its tie event is null by the independent last coordinate.\nThe scalar implementation uses a measurable Bool spin equal to that\nsign almost surely. It follows that\n$$\nS_{T,N}=\\sum_{j=1}^N A_jB_{j+1}\\le P_*.\n$$\nThis finite inequality assumes admissibility, $0\\le T<1$, $N>0$ and\npositive active second moments, but no minimizer or diffusion.\n[Literal scalar specialization][scalar-literal-lower].\n\nAt a minimizer, the separate contact and diffusion arguments establish\nthe fixed-strip inputs and eventual positivity. First take the mesh\nlimit to obtain $I(T)\\le P_*$, then let $T\\uparrow1$. The scalar\nterminal assembly identifies that last limit with $\\mathcal P(\\gamma)$;\ntogether with $P_*\\le\\mathcal P(\\gamma)$ this proves equality.\nThe third scalar endpoint constructs its own diffusion before making\nthis application. These sources therefore supply an independent route\nto the equality, alongside the imported Parisi formula in §6.\n[Contact assembly][scalar-contact-assembly]. The inspection here does\nnot change the research replay outcome in §13.\n","span":{"start_byte":178219,"end_byte":180612,"start_line":3807,"end_line":3858},"paper_href":"/api/v1/papers/long?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb","reading_href":"/api/v1/papers/reading?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&paper=long&passage=gaussian-expectation-limit","query_href":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&paper=long&passage=gaussian-expectation-limit"},{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:9e0f6d255eb0a116c513a0c11e94d28559f0a429ef365c3137bb4ff3b6a061d6/papers/long.md/sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json/passages/heat-lower-bound","anchor":"heat-lower-bound","mapping_ref":"sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids","text":"\n<a id=\"heat-lower-bound\"></a>\n\n**Corollary 10.2 (the heat test).** In this normalization,\n$$\nP_*\\ge\\frac{4\\Gamma(3/4)}{\\sqrt\\pi\\,\\Gamma(1/4)}\n       \\approx0.7627597635.\n\\tag{10.2}\n$$\nFor each fixed $T<1$ and $N\\ge1$, the heat normalizers $c_j$ are\npositive. The increments $g_j$ have unit second moments and are\northogonal, since their multipliers depend only on preceding Gaussians.\nThe rounded $F_N$ is a measurable spin after an arbitrary choice on its\nnull tie event. Thus Proposition 10.1 applies with\n$K=N$, $z_j=Z_{j+1}$ and $f_j=g_{j+1}$, giving $S_{T,N}\\le P_*$.\nFirst let $N\\to\\infty$ at fixed $T$, using the displayed exact sum,\nand then let $T\\uparrow1$. The integrand is nonnegative and its endpoint\nsingularity has exponent $1/4<1$. Substitution $s=t^2$ yields\n$$\nP_*\\ge\\frac2\\pi\\int_0^1(1-t^2)^{-1/4}\\,dt\n =\\frac1\\pi B\\!\\left(\\frac12,\\frac34\\right)\n =\\frac{4\\Gamma(3/4)}{\\sqrt\\pi\\,\\Gamma(1/4)}.\n$$\nThis is a consequence of the heat calculation and the general Gaussian\ncomparison. Minimality is unnecessary for the inequality; it is what\nmade the different, optimizing construction converge to $P_*$ itself.\n","span":{"start_byte":186643,"end_byte":187768,"start_line":4017,"end_line":4042},"paper_href":"/api/v1/papers/long?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb","reading_href":"/api/v1/papers/reading?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&paper=long&passage=heat-lower-bound","query_href":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&paper=long&passage=heat-lower-bound"}],"lenses":{"prerequisites":"/api/v1/graph?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&view=arguments&node=scalar.source_predictable_lower&assumptions=true","consumers":"/api/v1/graph?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&view=arguments&node=scalar.source_predictable_lower&direction=consumers&assumptions=true","direct_passages":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&node=scalar.source_predictable_lower&relation=direct","prerequisite_uses":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&node=scalar.source_predictable_lower&relation=prerequisites"},"relations":[{"family":"arguments","id":"sha256:c941bb8e2efcb2c545fdebbfec6f904193275455192bec48bcb452c5b8ab7f97/edges/%5B%22scalar.source_predictable_lower%22%2C%22scalar.source_empirical_energy_limit%22%2C%22supports%22%5D","from":"sha256:c941bb8e2efcb2c545fdebbfec6f904193275455192bec48bcb452c5b8ab7f97/nodes/scalar.source_predictable_lower","to":"sha256:c941bb8e2efcb2c545fdebbfec6f904193275455192bec48bcb452c5b8ab7f97/nodes/scalar.source_empirical_energy_limit","kind":"supports","semantic_class":"curated_argument_support","derived":false,"record_ref":"sha256:c941bb8e2efcb2c545fdebbfec6f904193275455192bec48bcb452c5b8ab7f97/edges/%5B%22scalar.source_predictable_lower%22%2C%22scalar.source_empirical_energy_limit%22%2C%22supports%22%5D"},{"family":"arguments","id":"sha256:c941bb8e2efcb2c545fdebbfec6f904193275455192bec48bcb452c5b8ab7f97/edges/%5B%22scalar.source_predictable_lower%22%2C%22scalar.source_finite_adaptive_lower%22%2C%22supports%22%5D","from":"sha256:c941bb8e2efcb2c545fdebbfec6f904193275455192bec48bcb452c5b8ab7f97/nodes/scalar.source_predictable_lower","to":"sha256:c941bb8e2efcb2c545fdebbfec6f904193275455192bec48bcb452c5b8ab7f97/nodes/scalar.source_finite_adaptive_lower","kind":"supports","semantic_class":"curated_argument_support","derived":false,"record_ref":"sha256:c941bb8e2efcb2c545fdebbfec6f904193275455192bec48bcb452c5b8ab7f97/edges/%5B%22scalar.source_predictable_lower%22%2C%22scalar.source_finite_adaptive_lower%22%2C%22supports%22%5D"},{"family":"arguments","id":"sha256:c941bb8e2efcb2c545fdebbfec6f904193275455192bec48bcb452c5b8ab7f97/edges/%5B%22scalar.source_predictable_lower%22%2C